<!--
  Glass.xaml — GlassLevel material mapping + the NOW/spotlight radial-gradient primitive, ported
  from Volar/Sources/Design/Theme.swift's `enum GlassLevel` and `SpotlightBackground`
  (Sources/Design/Glass.swift's `GlassBackground` provides the same tint-over-material recipe).

  GlassLevel -> WinUI mapping:
    - The WINDOW's own backdrop uses Mica (MicaBackdrop/MicaAltBackdrop), set in code-behind on
      MainWindow (SystemBackdrop property) — Mica is the system material closest to macOS's
      per-window vibrancy and is set once per Window, not as a per-control brush, so it isn't a
      ResourceDictionary entry.
    - Per-control "glass" panels/cards (GlassBackground's use case: cornerRadius'd rounded-rect
      panels layered on TOP of the Mica-backed window) use AcrylicBrush below, tinted with
      VolarBg at the same TintOpacity as Theme.swift's `GlassLevel.bgOpacity` (subtle .92 /
      standard .78 / heavy .55) — the SwiftUI code layers `tint.opacity(level.bgOpacity)` over a
      system Material; AcrylicBrush's TintColor+TintOpacity is the direct WinUI equivalent.

  NOTE — blur radius fidelity: Theme.swift's `GlassLevel.blur` (14/24/36) is explicitly
  documentation-only there too ("kept for documentation/fidelity even though SwiftUI's system
  Materials don't take an explicit blur radius parameter"). WinUI's AcrylicBrush/Mica likewise
  do not expose a settable blur radius — this is a structural material-system difference on
  BOTH platforms, not a Windows-side gap, so no numeric value is lost by omitting it here.
-->
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- ============================================================
         Per-control glass/acrylic brushes (GlassBackground equivalents).
         TintColor = VolarBg (#07090E). TintOpacity = GlassLevel.bgOpacity for that level.
         FallbackColor = VolarSurface, used when the OS disables transparency effects.
         ============================================================ -->

    <!-- GlassLevel.subtle: bgOpacity 0.92. NOTE: WinUI 3's Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Media.AcrylicBrush
         has no BackgroundSource property (that's a UWP-only Windows.UI.Xaml.Media.AcrylicBrush
         member) — WinUI's AcrylicBrush always composites against the window backdrop, so there
         is nothing to set here. -->
    <AcrylicBrush
        x:Key="VolarGlassSubtleBrush"
        TintColor="{StaticResource VolarBg}"
        TintOpacity="0.92"
        FallbackColor="{StaticResource VolarSurface}" />

    <!-- GlassLevel.standard (default): bgOpacity 0.78 -->
    <AcrylicBrush
        x:Key="VolarGlassStandardBrush"
        TintColor="{StaticResource VolarBg}"
        TintOpacity="0.78"
        FallbackColor="{StaticResource VolarSurface}" />

    <!-- GlassLevel.heavy: bgOpacity 0.55 -->
    <AcrylicBrush
        x:Key="VolarGlassHeavyBrush"
        TintColor="{StaticResource VolarBg}"
        TintOpacity="0.55"
        FallbackColor="{StaticResource VolarSurface}" />

    <!-- Hairline border brush for glass panels (GlassBackground's default borderColor). -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="VolarGlassBorderBrush" Color="{StaticResource VolarBorderHi}" />

    <!-- ============================================================
         NEW: NOW / spotlight primitive (Theme.swift SpotlightBackground / menubar-now.html
         `.pool` + `.vignette`). Reserved for exactly ONE (the active/NOW) task container at a
         time — "if two things on a screen are amber, one of them is wrong."

         SpotlightBackground layers a RadialGradient(nowGlow -> nowGlowSoft -> clear) as a
         background `.pool`, blurred. WinUI's RadialGradientBrush has no blur parameter (same
         structural gap as GlassLevel.blur above); the falloff is instead carried entirely by
         the 3-stop gradient below, which is a closer match to a blurred pool than a 2-stop one.
         ============================================================ -->
    <RadialGradientBrush x:Key="NowSpotlightBrush" Center="0.5,0.5" RadiusX="1" RadiusY="1">
        <GradientStop Offset="0.0" Color="{StaticResource VolarNowGlow}" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.55" Color="{StaticResource VolarNowGlowSoft}" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1.0" Color="Transparent" />
    </RadialGradientBrush>

    <!-- NOW-specific focus ring / hairline accent (nowRing, amber-ring @0.55 alpha) — e.g. a
         Border.BorderBrush around the single spotlighted task's container or an "m-chip.warm"
         style chip border. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NowRingBrush" Color="{StaticResource VolarNowRing}" />

    <!-- ============================================================
         NEW (Wave 4 Stage A — wave4-contract.md deliverable 5 / views-inventory.md §0 & §2 item 6):
         the SpotlightBackground `.overlay` half (Theme.swift:296-308) — a dark radial vignette,
         off-axis at UnitPoint(0.5, 0.32), that settles the `.pool` gradient's edges into cool
         shadow. Consumers: TodayView's NOW spotlight card, PopoverView's transcript-region
         spotlight (both per views-inventory.md §0's flagged gap — neither view was buildable with
         full visual fidelity until this brush existed).

         SwiftUI's `RadialGradient(colors: [.clear, VolarColor.bg.opacity(0.45)], center:
         UnitPoint(0.5, 0.32), startRadius: 140, endRadius: 320)` uses ABSOLUTE point radii;
         WinUI's RadialGradientBrush (like NowSpotlightBrush above) maps gradient-stop offsets to a
         RELATIVE 0..1 space over the element's own bounding box. Converted the two radii to a
         stop-offset ratio instead: startRadius/endRadius = 140/320 = 0.4375 -> the first (Transparent)
         stop sits at offset 0.4375 (everything inside that radius stays unpainted/transparent, same
         as SwiftUI's "nothing renders before startRadius" behavior), and the second (bg @0.45) stop
         sits at offset 1.0 (RadiusX/RadiusY = 1, i.e. the element's own edge = SwiftUI's endRadius).
         This is a relative-vs-absolute approximation, same structural caveat already called out for
         GlassLevel.blur/NowSpotlightBrush above — exact only when the hosting element's bounding box
         happens to be square-ish and sized so its edge roughly lines up with 320pt; visually close
         enough for a vignette (a soft, low-frequency gradient) that exact pixel radii don't matter.

         VolarColor.bg (#07090E) @0.45 -> alpha = round(0.45*255) = 114.75 -> 115 = 0x73 -> #7307090E.
         No pre-existing named color for this exact alpha exists in Colors.xaml (bg's other alpha
         variants are all accent/priority tokens, not this one), so it's inlined here rather than
         inventing a new Colors.xaml key used nowhere else — same "component-local one-off" pattern
         Components.swift's own inline `Color.white.opacity(...)` literals already establish. -->
    <RadialGradientBrush x:Key="NowSpotlightVignetteBrush" Center="0.5,0.32" RadiusX="1" RadiusY="1">
        <GradientStop Offset="0.4375" Color="Transparent" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1.0" Color="#7307090E" />
    </RadialGradientBrush>

</ResourceDictionary>
